good morning labour is coming under  pressure from the unions and some mps  after shadow minister sam terry was  sacked from the front bench after he  joined railway workers on the picket  line labour says he was removed for  making unauthorized tv appearances  including this interview with us  yesterday morning here's a reminder of  what he had to say  i've not been instant normal to anyone i  support kia osama i think he's by far  the best person to be prime minister of  this country i believe he will be the  prime minister of this country my job  today is to support transport workers as  a shadow transport minister to support  the traveling public every worker  deserves a pay rise that is in line with  inflation and at the moment the rmt and  tsa have been offered four percent this  year when inflation's running at 11 and  then at 2 on the following two years  that is a real terms pay cut no worker  should be offered a pay cut as a way  forward whilst having a sort of gun put  to their head in terms of losing their  jobs  yesterday um kia stammer was watching he  didn't like what he saw john mcdonald  party's former shadow chancellor is with  us hello to you thanks for joining us on  the program what did you make of what's  happened over the last 24 hours  i don't know who's advising kirsten but  this is a completely unnecessary row  that's been invented just at a time when  the tories are tearing themselves apart  and we've got the maximum opportunity i  think to gain an advantage in the polls  that will take us build the support to  take us into a government we're having  this completely unnecessary row it's  as i said sam went on the picket lines  like  well like  minister after minister shadow minnesota  shadow minister over the years  in support of workers who are asking for  a decent pay rise it's a just cause i  mean  and now we're told he's been sacked not  because he went on the picket lines but  because he made statements on the picket  lines but what was he supposed to do go  on there wear a gag it's a it's a silly  silly situation to get into so i think  we just need to accept that there will  be a wave of industrial action now  inevitably from union after union  because their members are voting  overwhelmingly for industrial action  because they're saying we can't accept a  pay cut and we've got to come off the  fence and be on the side of well adjust  cause the workers i think they've got it  right so it's this is an unnecessary  dispute and i i regret whoever's advised  kirsten on this i think has made it a  severe mistake  um he was sacked rotel because he was  making up policy on the fly it's not for  a junior shadow minister to make up the  policy of the opposition party  well he's expressed a view about the  dispute which i think is the correct  view that's the first thing but you know  rachel reeves went on an interview last  week and made up policy on rail  nationalization which had to be  contradicted by the shadow spokesperson  on transport within hours i didn't see  rachel reeves being sacked  i just think this is an unnecessary  division when we should be united and  you know the tory leadership election is  demonstrating how the tories are ripping  themselves apart this is the time when  we should have maximum unity a maximum  solidarity for the support for our  workers who are desperate to have a  decent pay rise because they're being  impacted upon so badly by this cost of  living crisis what do you want the  leader to do he tells them not to go on  the picket lines they do anyway some of  them are disciplined and let me finish  sanctioned and then they do it again  he's told him not to and they do it  again not only does he do that as a you  know he was the most senior of of the  labour mps that was there he's all be a  junior uh shadow minister not only does  he do that he then goes on the telly  does a round of media rounds and  disagrees with the policy of the party  what do you want him to do  well  i think the advisers around here have  dug us into a hole in the first place  unnecessarily by telling people not to  go on picket lines the reality is over  the years we're a trade we were formed  by the trade unions so when the trade  unions have a just cause we support them  and this is a just cause people have  been impacted upon by the cost of living  crisis they don't want a wage cut  they're trying to negotiate but they're  being forced to take industrial action  so you back them that's what we did in  the p o dispute you know the labor party  came out in favor of the workers there  because it was a just cause now to dig  ourselves into a hole like this telling  people they can't go on picket lines was  inevitably going to lead to something  like this a a real mistaken division  like this i think it's time to back off  and recognize the real issue is what  working-class people are going through  at the moment which is a terrible cost  of living crisis how do we solve it we  back them by getting inflation approved  pay awards to support them it's as  simple as that so i i think mistakes  have been made from the very beginning  we need to stand back and actually start  trying to secure unity across not just  the whole labor and trade union movement  but across the country overall i think  kia and his advisors have completely  misread the situation i think they've  misread the mood within the labor and  trade union movement but i also think  they've misread the mood amongst the  general public who also there's millions  of people out there who are now  contemplating and voting for industrial  action because they can't survive on the  wages and the wage offer that they've  been given so we've got to back them  because it's the just cause okay so what  would you say to those that suggest that  actually what he's trying to do is make  the party electable which is what you  and jeremy corbyn didn't manage to do  well  because what we're trying to do now is  make the party electable and one way of  making it electable is making sure you  secure the support of those millions of  people out there who like us think  actually it's wrong to impose a wage cut  on people when there's a cost of living  crisis like this and what we're doing  now is alienating people when the  election comes you know people will  rightfully say where were you when we  needed you when we were campaigning to  prevent our wages being cut that's all  that this is about i actually i do think  they've misread the public mood i've  certainly misread the mood within the  labour and trade union i've never seen  this level of anger but also you know  this level of solidarity wave after wave  of trade unions now are balloting  they're getting overwhelming results  people turning out in large numbers and  90 voted for industrial action we've  never seen that before why because  people are pretty angry and they're  saying we can't live on these wages well  they were given the opportunity of  course weren't they um back in 2019 mr  mcdonald and you presided over with  jeremy corbyn the biggest loss for  labour since 1935 but as you learned  those lessons it was a brexit general  election simple as that we've moved on  since then and the issues that people  are facing now are  tough it's a tough economic environment  out there where people have had 12 years  of  pay freezes and pay pay cuts they're now  hit with inflation going up to 11  and some of them are being offered two  or three percent wage increases which  means a wage cut times have moved on  since december 19 what we have to  recognize is that people are suffering  out there and we've got to be seen to be  on their side and one way of being seen  to be on their side is when they take  industrial action you support them you  join the picket lines and you back them  up it's a basic principle of solidarity  not just in the labor and trade union  boom but actually i think it's at the  heart of our society as well where you  support people when they've got a just  pause  rmt union chief mick lynch yesterday on  the program calling for a general strike  would you support him in that  well what's happening now what's  interesting if you look at the ballots  that are taking place across the trade  junior movement we talk about millions  of workers now voting for industrial  action so naturally what people are  saying is well why not coordinate that  well i support coordinated action  because if that results then you know  decent pay rise for people they're  protected against the cost of living  crisis i think that's the most effective  thing to do but you know this is  completely unnecessary what it needs is  the government to recognize that you  can't expect people to ex to have a well  to stand on one side when their wages  are being cut you know it's tough out  there people are struggling to survive  keep up with  their rents their mortgages putting food  on the table for their children we can't  go on like this we've got to protect  people and that's why people are saying  well we're going to have to take  industrial action and if that means  coordinated action to make it more  effective and to resolve the disputes  that's much more quickly more so be it  so you would support circle for a  general strike we haven't had one of  those since 1928.  well i don't i don't think it would be  necessary if we had a government that  recognized just how tough it is for  people out there you can't expect people  to have a pay cut when prices are going  up so well 11 and you know for some  people that's even higher so you can  understand why people are angry you can  understand why they vote for industrial  action and you can understand why unions  are saying if we are going to strike we  might as well coordinate that action so  coordinated action i think is going to  be inevitable and i just hope the  government starts listening  if there are picket lines that are  caused as 